# XSH tcgetsid()
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# A controlling terminal is built in a session of its own: a child calls setsid() and opens
# the pseudo-terminal subsidiary without O_NOCTTY. Acquisition that way is
# implementation-defined (XBD 11.1.3), so the test is UNRESOLVED when the pathname from
# ctermid() cannot be opened afterwards. The session ID is the process group ID of the
# session leader, which for a leader is its process ID.

assertion  XSH.tcgetsid.001
anchor     functions/tcgetsid.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/tcgetsid.html#RETURN VALUE
class      conditional
when       BASE|XSI
since      2008
says       In a session leader whose controlling terminal is a pseudo-terminal,
           tcgetsid() on the subsidiary descriptor returns the process group ID
           of the session, which is the leader's own.
note       XSI only for the pseudo-terminal set-up (posix_openpt() and its
           companions are XSI); the requirement itself is Base. A set-up that is
           not available is UNRESOLVED, or NOTINUSE when the facility is absent.
timeout    40
test       t/001-leader.c

assertion  XSH.tcgetsid.002
anchor     functions/tcgetsid.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/tcgetsid.html#RETURN VALUE
class      conditional
when       BASE|XSI
since      2008
says       tcgetsid() called by a process in a background process group of the
           session returns the same value: the session leader's process group ID.
note       XSI only for the pseudo-terminal set-up (posix_openpt() and its
           companions are XSI); the requirement itself is Base. A set-up that is
           not available is UNRESOLVED, or NOTINUSE when the facility is absent.
timeout    40
test       t/002-from-member.c

assertion  XSH.tcgetsid.003
anchor     functions/tcgetsid.html#ERRORS/EBADF
class      conditional
when       BASE|XSI
since      2008
says       In a process that has a controlling terminal, tcgetsid(-1) and
           tcgetsid() of a closed descriptor return -1 with EBADF.
note       XSI only for the pseudo-terminal set-up (posix_openpt() and its
           companions are XSI); the requirement itself is Base. A set-up that is
           not available is UNRESOLVED, or NOTINUSE when the facility is absent.
           The call is made in a process with a controlling terminal so that
           ENOTTY does not also apply.
timeout    40
test       t/003-ebadf.c

assertion  XSH.tcgetsid.004
anchor     functions/tcgetsid.html#ERRORS/ENOTTY
class      conditional
when       BASE|XSI
since      2008
says       In a process that has a controlling terminal, tcgetsid() of a regular
           file, both ends of a pipe and /dev/null returns -1 with ENOTTY.
note       XSI only for the pseudo-terminal set-up (posix_openpt() and its
           companions are XSI); the requirement itself is Base. A set-up that is
           not available is UNRESOLVED, or NOTINUSE when the facility is absent.
timeout    40
test       t/004-enotty-file.c

assertion  XSH.tcgetsid.005
anchor     functions/tcgetsid.html#ERRORS/ENOTTY
class      conditional
when       BASE|XSI
since      2008
says       In a process that has no controlling terminal (it called setsid() and
           opened the pseudo-terminal subsidiary with O_NOCTTY), tcgetsid() on
           that descriptor returns -1 with ENOTTY.
note       XSI only for the pseudo-terminal set-up (posix_openpt() and its
           companions are XSI); the requirement itself is Base. A set-up that is
           not available is UNRESOLVED, or NOTINUSE when the facility is absent.
timeout    40
test       t/005-no-ctty.c

assertion  XSH.tcgetsid.006
anchor     functions/tcgetsid.html#ERRORS/ENOTTY
class      conditional
when       BASE|XSI
since      2008
says       In a process whose controlling terminal is one pseudo-terminal,
           tcgetsid() on the subsidiary descriptor of another pseudo-terminal
           (opened with O_NOCTTY) returns -1 with ENOTTY.
note       XSI only for the pseudo-terminal set-up (posix_openpt() and its
           companions are XSI); the requirement itself is Base. A set-up that is
           not available is UNRESOLVED, or NOTINUSE when the facility is absent.
timeout    40
test       t/006-other-tty.c
